{{smaller|'''Back of a window.''' See ''back''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Blind window.''' S ''blind''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Clustered window,''' a window consisting three or more lights grouped together. Examples especially frequent in medieval architecture.—'''Coupl windows, dormant window†, false window, fa shaped window.''' See the adjectives, and cuts un ''coupled windows'' and ''dormer-window''.—'''French windo''' a window having two sashes hinged at the sides, a opening in the middle.—'''Goldsmiths’ window,''' a v rich claim in which the gold shows freely. [Mining sla Australia.]—'''House out of windows†.''' See ''house''<sup>1</sup> '''Jesse window.''' See ''Jesse''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Lattice-window.''' See ''l tice'', 2 (with cut).—'''Low side window.''' Same as ''lych scope''.—'''Oriel-window.''' See ''oriel'' (with cut).—'''Stool a window.''' See ''stool''.—'''Venetian window,''' a wind which has three separate lights.—'''Window tax, wind duty,''' a tax formerly levied in Great Britain on windo of houses, latterly on all in excess of six in number. was abolished in 1851, a tax on houses above a cert rental being substituted. (See also ''dormer-window'', ''lan window'', ''rose-window'', ''wheel-window''.)}}

'''window''' (win′dō), ''v. t.'' [< ''window'', ''n.''] '''1.''' furnish with a window or with windows.
{{smaller block|Within a ''window’d'' niche of that high hall<br>Sate Brunswick’s fated chieftain.
{{right|''Byron'', Childe Harold, iii.}}}}
'''2.''' To make openings or rents in.
{{smaller block|Your loop’d and ''window’d'' raggedness.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, iii. 4.}}}}
'''3.''' To place in a window.
{{smaller block|Wouldst thou be ''window’d'' in great Rome and see<br>Thy master thus? ''Shak.'', A. and C., iv. 14.}}

